---
epr-meta-version: 1
id: bridges-pkarr-integrity-coupled
covers: subtree
purpose: >
  pkarr-bridge: deterministic, pkarr-shaped signed endpoint records for doorway discovery. The
  infrastructure DHT is the truth and pkarr publication is a later projection. The crate is
  compiled into the infrastructure DNA's integrity zome (and its coordinator), so its code is
  part of that DNA's hash.
rules:
  - id: integrity-zome-dependency-moves-dna-hash
    class: inject
    when: { write: "*.rs", new: true }
    dedupe-of: "elohim/holochain/dna/infrastructure/zomes/infrastructure_integrity/Cargo.toml (the path dependency that compiles this crate into the integrity zome) + elohim/holochain/dna/.epr-meta/manifest.md (dna-hash-neutrality: what a hash-moving change costs)"
    why: >
      This crate is compiled into the infrastructure DNA's integrity zome, so a change to the code
      that zome compiles moves that DNA's hash, and a new module joins that build as soon as it is
      declared. A moved hash does not reach running conductors on a normal redeploy. It lands only through a
      deliberate DNA-lineage event (ALLOW_DNA_REINSTALL), which mints a new agent key, and peers
      reinstalled on different hashes split into different DHTs. If the new code is only needed
      where records are created or read, put it in the infrastructure coordinator zome or the
      doorway side, which hot-swap without touching the hash. If it must validate, plan it as part
      of a lineage event for the whole namespace.
    retire-when: >
      when the record-validation surface the integrity zome needs is split into its own crate, so
      editing the rest of pkarr-bridge no longer touches the infrastructure DNA's hash
---
# bridges/pkarr — signed endpoint records

The one hazard here is structural and easy to miss from inside the crate: it lives under
`bridges/`, but its bytes are part of the infrastructure DNA hash. The rule fires when a new
`.rs` file is born, which is where new logic most often enters. It advises and never blocks.
Edits to existing files are not flagged; the DNA pipeline and the hash-neutrality signal in
`elohim/holochain/dna/` carry that part.
